<!--
The single canonical entry for the beet website, and the one place its cloud
resources are declared. Discovery loads it locally when the `beet` binary runs
from the `site/` directory (`cd site && beet serve --server=http`), and the
deployed Lightsail box loads the same entry directly from the app bucket root.
The workspace commands that take an entry as an argument (`beet check site`,
`beet export-static site`) load it from the repo root.

The root is a one-shot `<CliServer always=true {CallOnReady}>` dispatcher whose
`Router` is its child, so the entry is a set of commands and the served site is ONE
of them: `serve`. `always=true` makes the dispatcher run on every boot regardless of
`--server`, since `--server` selects the *served site's* transports, not this
entry's. The box launches `app --store=s3://.. --server=http,ssh serve` and a
developer launches `beet --main=site serve --server=http`: one process, one
argv, the same verb, so local and deployed take the same path.

    beet --main=site serve --server=http      # the website over http
    beet --main=site serve --server=tui       # ..as a live terminal (like `beet present`)
    beet --main=site serve --server=http,ssh  # ..http plus a multi-tenant ssh terminal

A `Router` is a url space, so the site's own routes stay rooted at `/` despite
hanging off the `serve` command, and no http/ssh request can reach a sibling
command route.

## One declaration, two meanings

`<DynamoTableBlock label="analytics"/>` is declared once, here, at router level.
The DEPLOY provisions it into the stage stack (a block outside any deploy host
is adopted by the host carrying this process's stage), and the RUNTIME attaches
a store for it, which the content router names with `StoreRef($analytics)`.
Both sides compose `<app>--<stage>--analytics` through the one `ResourceScope`,
from the `app_name` on this entry's `<PackageConfig/>`, so they cannot disagree.
The live site once spent a whole deploy writing to a table that did not exist
because the two sides derived that name independently.

## The served site

`<Route path="serve" {(HttpServer, TuiServer, SshTuiServer)}>` is the serve route
and the server host in one: dispatching it calls the route's `RunningSet`, which
parks and starts the three long-running servers (`HttpServer`, the local live
`TuiServer`, and the multi-tenant `SshTuiServer`). They dispatch into the content
`Router` child, whose middleware and layout attach as component spreads: request
logging, analytics, `--help`, terminal link navigation, the layout, and the cache
policy (`CacheHeadersMiddleware` + `CacheHeaders` mark html pages edge-cacheable,
which the Cloudflare proxy honors and the deploy/sync purge refreshes).
`<DefaultAppRoutes/>` adds the default app routes (`/app-info`, the reactivity
runtime, …), `<RoutesDir/>` discovers the `routes/` pages, and `<AssetsDir
src="assets" cache="1h"/>` mounts the assets the site and blog reference,
browser-cacheable for an hour.

`site/assets` is a real directory whose source of record is the site's own shared
bucket (`beet-site--shared--assets`), hydrated with `just site-shared pull`. A
few files it serves are BUILT in the workspace tree (the wasm binary the tutorial
embeds, the geoip database, the perceive-act robot faces); `<DirCopy/>` is the
one declaration of what is borrowed, and runs ahead of every publish.

## The deploy

Structure is universal, so this same file loads whole in the deployed binary
(built `--no-default-features --features aws_sdk,ssh,geoip`): the deploy tags it
never linked build as inert entities, quietly, because the host declares
`RequireFeatures("infra,extra")`. That declaration is also the loudness:
dispatching any deploy verb in a binary without those features fails naming
them and the rebuild that fixes it, before any step runs. That is what lets the
application declare its own infrastructure without shipping the deployer.

The deploy route applies once per layer, and the order is the point: `<TofuApply
layer="storage"/>` brings up the stores (the app bucket, the analytics table),
`<DirSync/>` fills them, and a bare `<TofuApply/>` converges the whole stack:
it uploads the built binary under the deploy id, publishes the release pointer
naming it, and converges the instance against a bucket that already holds the
site. One apply boots the new instance first, so it finds an empty bucket and no
entry document. Naming a layer no block declares is a loud error, never a silent
skip.

Most deploys do NOT replace the box. The instance's cloud-init renders machine
config only (Caddy, the sshd relocation, the CloudWatch agent, the unit), never a
version, so a code-only deploy plans no change to it; `<LightsailRelease/>` then
reaches the box over the management sshd on 2222 and rolls the unit onto the new
binary, verifying the RUNNING process reports this deploy's id. A machine-config
change still replaces the box, and rotates its access key with it. Either way the
static IP re-attaches so DNS never moves, and the ssh host key rides the env
channel so the fingerprint never changes.

Cloudflare fronts the box: http hostnames are proxied and edge-cached `A` records
at the static IP, ssh (and future live apps) ride the DNS-only `app.beet.org` /
`app.dev.beet.org` hostnames straight to it, `<CloudflareZoneSetup/>` converges
the zone-level edge config, and every deploy or sync ends with a
`<CloudflarePurgeCache/>`. On the deploy route the purge comes *after*
`<LightsailWatch/>`, since purging while the old instance still serves just
re-caches the old responses for the full edge TTL.

The assets bucket lives on its own `shared` stage under the `shared/` route
prefix, provisioned as its own step since a source shared by developers is a
different process from a stage deploy. No stage deploy or destroy touches it.

    just beet-deploy [--stage=prod]        # build -> stores -> sync -> release -> watch -> purge
    just beet-sync                         # re-publish the site (no redeploy)
    just beet-watch                        # tail the instance logs
    just site-shared apply                 # provision the site assets bucket
    just site-shared pull                  # hydrate site/assets from it (no credentials)
    just site-shared push                  # publish site/assets to it

Live deploy needs AWS credentials + `BEET_SSH_HOST_KEY`/`CLOUDFLARE_ZONE_ID`/
`CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN` in `.env`, plus `tofu` and `cargo-zigbuild`.
-->
<CliServer always=true {CallOnReady}>
<Router {HelpHandler}>
	<!-- the ONE location app identity lives: the deploy names its resources from
	     this, and so does the running binary. -->
	<PackageConfig title="Beet" app_name="beet-site" description="The beet website, built with beet" version="0.0.9-dev.16"/>
	<!-- declared once: the deploy creates it, the runtime records to it -->
	<DynamoTableBlock bx:ref="analytics" label="analytics"/>
	<Route path="serve" {(HttpServer, TuiServer, SshTuiServer)}>
		<Router {(RequestLogger, AnalyticsMiddleware, AnalyticsConfig, StoreRef($analytics), CacheHeadersMiddleware, CacheHeaders, HelpHandler, NavigateHandler, BsxLayout{template:"Layout"})}>
			<Theme color={Srgba(Srgba{red:0.0, green:1.0, blue:0.75, alpha:1.0})}/>
			<TemplateDir src="templates"/>
			<Styles/>
			<DefaultAppRoutes/>
			<RoutesDir src="routes"/>
			<AssetsDir src="assets" cache="1h"/>
		</Router>
	</Route>
	<DeployHost {RequireFeatures("infra,extra")}>
		<Route path="deploy" {ExchangeSequence}>
			<!-- the block + its build artifact share one entity (the deploy pairs them) -->
			<LightsailBeetSiteBlock features="aws_sdk,ssh,geoip"/>
			<S3BucketBlock label="app" deploy_versioned=false/>
			<TofuApply layer="storage"/>
			<DirCopy src="assets" dest="site/assets" paths="extra/robot-eyes,databases/country.mmdb,wasm/beet-full.wasm"/>
			<DirSync bucket="app" local_dir="site" {SyncS3Bucket{delete:true}}/>
			<TofuApply/>
			<LightsailRelease/>
			<CloudflareZoneSetup/>
			<LightsailWatch timeout="300s"/>
			<CloudflarePurgeCache/>
		</Route>
		<Route path="sync" {ExchangeSequence}>
			<DirCopy src="assets" dest="site/assets" paths="extra/robot-eyes,databases/country.mmdb,wasm/beet-full.wasm"/>
			<DirSync bucket="app" local_dir="site" {SyncS3Bucket{delete:true}}/>
			<CloudflarePurgeCache/>
		</Route>
		<Route path="watch" {ExchangeSequence}>
			<LightsailWatch timeout="300s"/>
		</Route>
		<Route path="assets" {ExchangeSequence}>
			<DirCopy src="assets" dest="site/assets" paths="extra/robot-eyes,databases/country.mmdb,wasm/beet-full.wasm"/>
		</Route>
	</DeployHost>
	<Route path="shared">
		<DeployHost stage="shared" {RequireFeatures("infra,extra")}>
			<S3BucketBlock label="assets" deploy_versioned=false public_read=true force_destroy=false/>
			<Route path="push" {ExchangeSequence}>
				<DirCopy src="assets" dest="site/assets" paths="extra/robot-eyes,databases/country.mmdb,wasm/beet-full.wasm"/>
				<DirSync bucket="assets" local_dir="site/assets"/>
			</Route>
			<Route path="pull" {ExchangeSequence}>
				<DirSync bucket="assets" local_dir="site/assets" {SyncS3Bucket{direction:Pull, no_sign_request:true}}/>
			</Route>
		</DeployHost>
	</Route>
</Router>
</CliServer>
